              "Supporters of urban beekeeping argue that cities are, in many ways, surprisingly good places for bees. Urban areas contain a remarkable variety of flowering plants in parks, private gardens and cemeteries, and along roads and railway lines. Because these plants flower at different times, they provide food for much of the year. The countryside, by contrast, is often dominated by a few large crops that flower for only a few weeks. Cities are also largely free of the agricultural pesticides that have been linked to declines in bee health. Honey produced in cities has even won national prizes, and tests have generally found it to be free of harmful levels of pollution.",
