              "The evidence for older students is more mixed. A widely reported experiment in 2014 found that university students who took notes by hand during lectures performed better on conceptual questions afterwards than those who typed. The researchers suggested that because writing is slower, hand-writers are forced to summarise and rephrase, whereas typists tend to copy down the lecturer's words without thinking. Later attempts to repeat the experiment, however, produced weaker results, and some found no difference at all. I would not, therefore, use this research to argue that students should be banned from bringing laptops into lectures, as some universities have done. What it does suggest is that the method of taking notes matters less than whether students actually process what they hear.",
